WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.327
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.327 --> 00:00:07.660
GRACE MOLLISON: My name's Grace.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.564
I'm a Cloud Solutions
Architect based out

00:00:09.564 --> 00:00:11.980
the London office just in case
there's any confusion where

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.240
I came from.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.450
And I'm here to talk to you
about blending GCP security

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.940
controls.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.410
When it comes to
security, they're

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.080
kind of tends to
be two approaches--

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:26.590
thinking about what your
users are allowed to do

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.930
and also what's permitted
within your organization,

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:34.990
and then also protecting
against threats and concerns

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:39.050
and the controls you need
to do approach there.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:41.110
So as you would
expect with GCP, we

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:42.620
have a selection of controls.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.800
And these are just a subset.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:47.470
And this talk is all
about demystifying

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.470
how you can pick and choose
various controls to address

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:54.790
particular use cases starting
off with the first one, which

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.140
is what can your users do.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.120
When it comes to
permissions, IAM

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.140
dictates what your users can do.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.080
They dictate who can do
what on which resource.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.900
But before I go in to talk to
you about how you implement

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.390
IAM in GCP, I need to talk to
you about the Cloud Resource

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.180
Manager.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.810
The Cloud Resource
Manager allows

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.030
you to map your organization.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.060
It's a hierarchical
organization,

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.750
so you can map your teams or
maybe how your applications are

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.240
configured to map to GCP.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.910
In the context of
this talk, the thing

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.010
is it provides
hierarchical attachment

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:38.200
points for permissions
and organization policies.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.140
OK.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:43.280
So coming back to
IAM, when you use IAM,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.150
you have to create
an IAM policy.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.950
An IAM policy-- you bind
users to a set of permissions.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.600
And the set of permissions
are called roles.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.970
These are the users
that GCP recognizes,

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:58.421
but it doesn't preclude you
from bringing your own identity

00:01:58.421 --> 00:01:58.920
along.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.360
So you can use Active Directory.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.720
You can use LDAP.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.240
So once you've
defined your policy,

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.460
you then need to decide
where on the hierarchy

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.229
you need to attach it--

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.844
so whether at the organization
level, folder level--

00:02:11.844 --> 00:02:14.510
the folders have a collection of
projects within them-- project,

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.280
or even at the resource
level themselves.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.020
In GCP, as on-prem, you have
a number of privilege roles.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.250
You know, like your
super user accounts.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.340
So these are the ones that
you really, really need

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.350
to give least
privileges and restrict

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.830
the number of users who use
them, who are granted them.

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.280
So the policy admin
gives you that person

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.330
who has that broke
the ability to have

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.150
manager and policies all
throughout the organization.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.520
The organization
role admin gives them

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.770
the ability to create
custom roles that apply

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.720
throughout your organization.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.030
And Policy Admin folder
and project owner

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:58.500
are pretty self-evident.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.940
So what you need
to do is you need

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.550
to think about what roles
your users actually have.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.710
Are they central network
and security teams?

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.634
What permissions do they need?

00:03:12.634 --> 00:03:15.050
Your data scientists, they
don't have the same permissions

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.794
as your central teams and
network security teams.

00:03:17.794 --> 00:03:19.210
So you need to
think that through.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.330
So when it comes to
thinking about all of those,

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.860
you need to then kind of take
a path to implement that.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.540
Look at your
organization hierarchy.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:32.830
Do that mapping.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.330
Create that first.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:37.040
Do the functional to IAM map.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:37.540
OK.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.581
So that's probably a
spreadsheet where you train,

00:03:39.581 --> 00:03:41.530
OK, I know these
guys or these gals

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.190
need to be able to do that.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:46.090
Then determine what you
need to attach that policy.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.599
And you need to
audit all of this.

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:49.390
So this is going to be
an underlying theme,

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:52.410
the auditing and logging.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:53.000
OK.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.740
So let's just talk
through an example,

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.450
so you can understand
how this actually works.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:01.710
So here we have a central
security and networking

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.500
team in this organization.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.420
So they manage all the
network and security policies.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.550
They've given teach team of
developers and developers

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:12.650
their own projects,
but they're not

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.260
allowed to make any changes
to the network and security

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:15.920
control.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.000
They are allowed to
spin up instances.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.120
So before I go on to
look at the IAM policies,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.250
I just need to introduce
you to yet another control.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.350
This is shared VPC.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:33.320
This allows you to define an
RFC 1918 network, a network

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.760
network, which you can
divide up into subnets.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:38.660
These subnets, the
service projects--

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.000
i.e. the projects that you've
given to your developers--

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.030
can then make use of.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.310
OK?

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.670
So there are actually
three IAM policies

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:51.564
that you need for this
particular example.

00:04:51.564 --> 00:04:52.980
They're are three,
because they're

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:56.110
attached to different levels
in the hierarchy and projects.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:56.610
OK.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:59.440
So I've just talked you
through some of these.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.410
So the first one is attached
at the organization hierarchy

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:04.600
level.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:07.432
This is for the network
and security admins.

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:09.390
So there's two groups
here-- the network admin,

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.760
so you've got managing
all the network controls

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.840
and the security teams that are
managing the security teams--

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:16.690
oh, the security roles, sorry.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:17.190
OK.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:18.890
And this is managed at the--

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:23.160
attached at the
organization level.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.320
First of developer projects--
this is on the host project.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.960
They need to be granted
network user, because they're

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.350
going to be using that
subnet that you're

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:34.660
going to give them access to
which is in the host project.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:35.160
OK.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.470
So they need to have network
user in the host project

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.750
as well.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.160
Second one is that the
service project-- i.e.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.420
the project that they spin
these instants up in--

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:47.600
they also need network
user there as well.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.141
And they also need the ability
to spin up an instance admin.

00:05:51.141 --> 00:05:53.396
OK?

00:05:53.396 --> 00:05:57.190
So blending them all
together, visually you

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:59.170
can see where the
network and security

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:04.270
admins have access to manage the
shared VPC network in the host

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:05.050
project.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.880
And you can see
where the developers

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.580
can use the subnets that's been
assigned to their projects.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:15.010
Then they can manage the
instances in their project.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:16.900
So looking at what you
can do, users can do,

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.010
there's Resource Manager to give
you the hierarchical attachment

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.830
points in central
management, Cloud

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:26.860
IAM for those
granulated permissions,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.250
and shared VPC which allows
you to have the central network

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:34.490
and administration.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:35.020
OK.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.110
You'll see this slide a lot.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:37.776
It's just kind of to
give you a reminder

00:06:37.776 --> 00:06:40.700
of where they're coming from
in the logical groupings.

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:43.340
So at this point, I'd actually
like to introduce Charlie

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.620
from Verily, who's going
to explain to you how

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:48.584
they actually implement this
within their organization.

00:06:48.584 --> 00:06:49.250
Thanks, Charlie.

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:51.041
CHARLIE AARONSON: Thank
you so much, Grace.

00:06:51.041 --> 00:06:51.940
Hi, everyone.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:52.920
Welcome.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.520
I hope you have enjoyed your
time at GCP Next thus far.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:07:00.050
My name is Charlie Aaronson, and
I'm a Technical Program Manager

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:02.570
working on Cloud Data
Security at Verily Life

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:06.140
Sciences, an Alphabet company.

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:08.120
As you heard from
Diane and Sundar

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.100
yesterday at
yesterday's keynote,

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:13.610
Google's mission is to organize
the world's information

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.910
and make it universally
accessible and useful.

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:21.500
Well, borne out of Alphabet's
Moonshot Factory, Google X,

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.230
Verily has the mission to
make the world's health

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.300
information useful,
so that people

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.755
can enjoy healthier lives.

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.760
As you might imagine,
working towards this mission

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.280
can often take a
number of forms.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:40.420
And many times, we realized this
mission through partnerships.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:43.990
For some of these
partnerships, such as Verb,

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:47.320
focusing on surgical
robotics, or on Duo,

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:51.940
where we work on evolving
diabetes management, GCP

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:56.260
and, indeed, G suite are at
the core of our collaboration

00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:01.220
in terms of working together
as well as developing together.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:04.130
In order to ensure that
we're set up for success when

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:08.960
using GCP both internally and,
indeed, for these partnerships,

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.010
we've developed and follow a few
key principles for cloud data

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:15.170
security and operations.

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:17.510
These are separability.

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:20.950
We need to ensure that there
is a clear differentiation

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:24.440
in between different project
teams and their data.

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.870
Maintainability--
thinking through how

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:30.000
our systems are going to
be used not just today,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.640
but for years to come.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:34.820
And three-- simplicity,
ensuring that we

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.789
create and advise the use
of understandable security

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:40.034
configurations.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:46.580
And as you may imagine, bringing
these principles into action

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:51.440
often requires that we blend a
few different GCP and G Suite

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.211
tools and systems.

00:08:53.211 --> 00:08:54.710
Over the next few
minutes, I'm going

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:59.240
to explain how we use GCP
hierarchy, G Suite Groups, as

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.180
well as Cloud Identity
and Access Management,

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:06.940
IAM, to accomplish and
meet our principles.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:09.030
So let's start
with separability.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.430
We need to ensure that
our surgical analytics

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:15.540
data is entirely separate from
our baseline biological sample

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:16.620
data.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:20.160
We do this by organizing
our GCP hierarchy

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.850
into folders and then some
folders below that and project

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:24.840
below that.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.820
That allows, as you
can see on screen,

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.670
for us to literally
separate out the data

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.730
and the subsequent access
ensuring that we adhere

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.830
to the separability principle.

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:38.200
Next comes maintainability.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:42.220
We organize our
identities into groups

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.800
that are associated with
a specific role or project

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:46.270
within the organization.

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:48.160
So to give you a
tangible example,

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:50.650
think of Allison and Charlie,
who are both software

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.530
engineers on a given project.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.430
They're contained
within a group.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:56.080
And then the great
thing about this

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:58.720
is, if we have another software
engineer join the team,

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:01.690
we just add her to the
group, and she automatically

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:04.570
has all the access that
she needs to do a role.

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:06.430
It's a fantastic way
to ensure that we

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:11.500
maintain the level of groups
and access that we need.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.920
Next, rather than adding
individuals to resources,

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:19.030
we add these groups to resources
via either a canned widely

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:21.400
available or custom role.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:25.960
This allows us to focus on
ensuring that we get access

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:29.170
at the group level and make sure
that the appropriate people are

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:30.910
in the appropriate groups.

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:32.620
On a personal note,
this has saved me

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:35.890
an enormous amount of time in
troubleshooting access requests

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:38.620
and issues and is
fundamental to how

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.960
we approach maintainability.

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:44.030
Finally, comes simplicity.

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:45.950
By bringing together
these practices,

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:50.630
we work to create a consistent,
understandable, and, of course,

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:54.680
simple GCP access
control system at Verily.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:57.560
We endeavor to use the
platform as intended

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.690
rather than rolling
our own solutions.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:02.420
And we are thoughtful
about our hierarchy

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:05.810
and where we grant groups
access to resources

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:09.360
to ensure that we maintain, of
course, the appropriate access

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.600
as well as the
appropriate separation.

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:15.770
In using the
platform as intended,

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:18.790
we often find areas to
give feedback to GCP.

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:20.290
And this is fantastic.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:24.070
Our relationship with GCP has
been really collaborative.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:26.740
And this is not just because
we're an Alphabet Company.

00:11:26.740 --> 00:11:30.500
This is because GCP is
all about collaboration.

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:34.450
So I encourage you all, go ahead
and use your account teams,

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:37.120
your support representatives,
or product form

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:39.370
to ensure that you
provide feedback

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:41.710
on how you're using the
product to make it better

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:44.330
for all of us.

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:46.760
Data security and
segmentation are

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:49.220
of absolute paramount
importance to Verily.

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:52.460
Thus, in order to ensure that
our teams and collaborations

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:55.910
are set up for success, we
have developed principles

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:59.200
which guide our deployment of
Google Cloud Platform, GCP,

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:00.440
at Verily.

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:03.860
I hope I've been able to share
some brief insights about how

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:07.720
GCP is helping us realize our
mission of making the world's

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.090
health information useful, so
that people can enjoy healthier

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:11.960
lives.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:13.610
Thank you all so much.

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:16.352
And I'll pass you back to Grace.

00:12:16.352 --> 00:12:17.685
GRACE MOLLISON: Thanks, Charlie.

00:12:17.685 --> 00:12:21.130
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:22.740
Thank you very much.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:23.950
OK.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:26.620
So continuing on
with the use cases--

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:27.790
number two.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:30.710
Looking at enforcing,
what's not allowed?

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:32.440
There will often be
the case that you'll

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:38.140
want to have standard
controls that are spread down

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:39.280
throughout your org.

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:41.530
But you want to do
this without getting

00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:42.790
in the way of your developers.

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:44.800
You still want them to be agile.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.610
GCP has something called
organization policies.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.140
So the organization
policy service,

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:55.360
it allows you to provide
standard controls that

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.150
provide enforcement
throughout your organization

00:12:58.150 --> 00:13:00.561
that you can override
at different levels.

00:13:00.561 --> 00:13:01.060
OK?

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:04.980
So I'm just going to keep it
simple and talk about colors.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:08.470
But for every resource that
is supported by organization

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:10.510
policy, there's a
list of constraints

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:16.460
that tell you how that
resource can be restricted.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.670
So in this case, we're
looking at a resource

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:20.080
that's got four colors.

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:21.690
You may recognize them.

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:24.224
And the standard
here is the fact

00:13:24.224 --> 00:13:25.640
that you're only
going to have two

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:27.600
colors as the standard
colors that are

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.650
used within your organization.

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:31.860
So red and green,
this is your standard.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.570
But as ever, there's
always exceptions.

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:38.260
So the first group says, well,
OK, we like the standard,

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.120
but we also want to use blue.

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:41.400
So you just add blue.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:42.940
So you can augment it.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:44.950
The other one says, well,
we don't want our team

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:46.180
to be able to use green.

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:47.890
Well, I quite like green myself.

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:50.410
So they can only use red.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.870
And then the other
two, these standards

00:13:52.870 --> 00:13:55.130
aren't actually applying at all.

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:58.070
So in this case, they've
got an organization policy

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:00.010
that they've defined
at the folder level

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.440
for the first one,
which is just yellow.

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:06.790
And the last group, they're not
having standards apply to them.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:09.530
They can just use
whatever color they want.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:11.385
So you can see how
you can be adaptable.

00:14:11.385 --> 00:14:13.840
So you can set the
standard, but you can also

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.830
flex to meet the requirements.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:19.440
OK.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:25.290
So org policies and IAM
policies are orthogonal,

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:26.400
but slightly related.

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:28.740
So all policies are all
about how those resources can

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:29.880
be configured.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:33.982
Whereas, IAM policy is about
who can access those resources--

00:14:33.982 --> 00:14:39.960
OK, quite an
important distinction.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:43.370
So moving on to look at how
you can protect your GCP

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:47.220
environment, think of maybe like
preventing data exfiltration.

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:50.050
Think of that as being the use
case as I walk through this.

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:52.330
That'll help, OK?

00:14:52.330 --> 00:14:56.390
So as you'd expect, there is a
whole shopping list of controls

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:59.299
that you can use for
this particular use case.

00:14:59.299 --> 00:15:01.340
I'm just going to talk
about some of the controls

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:04.040
I haven't already touched upon.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:05.910
So let's start with subnets.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:09.600
Subnets allow you to
define a logical division

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:10.980
in terms of traffic.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:11.670
OK?

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.575
You can actually use
it to kind of separate,

00:15:13.575 --> 00:15:17.560
you know, your database back
end from your application

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:19.069
middleware.

00:15:19.069 --> 00:15:20.610
You can actually
have different teams

00:15:20.610 --> 00:15:22.707
working at different subnets.

00:15:22.707 --> 00:15:23.540
And you can use it--

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.230
when I say for HA
deployments, it's

00:15:25.230 --> 00:15:28.373
that you can, obviously, have
subnets within different zones.

00:15:28.373 --> 00:15:28.872
OK.

00:15:31.562 --> 00:15:33.020
Firewall-- the
firewall, obviously,

00:15:33.020 --> 00:15:36.680
helps you to manage traffic
between the subnets.

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:40.160
Because traffic, by
default, isn't actually

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:43.370
between the subnets unless
you specifically define it.

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.680
So the firewall is distributed.

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:49.720
It's up at the VPC network
that you define it.

00:15:49.720 --> 00:15:51.250
It has, you know,
as you'd expect,

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:55.242
ingress and egress rules,
deny and allow rules.

00:15:55.242 --> 00:15:57.450
One of the things that I
really, really like about it

00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:00.160
is the fact that you can
have [INAUDIBLE] rules.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:05.100
So just to understand that,
I'll just talk about firewalls

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:08.930
with looking at service
accounts to set up those rules.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:11.180
So service accounts, so
one of the identities

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:14.570
I touched upon
briefly early on, it's

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:19.590
the programmatic identity
for your VM or application.

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:23.370
So using service accounts allows
you to set up firewall rules

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:24.830
in terms of intent.

00:16:24.830 --> 00:16:27.180
i.e., rather than
thinking about, oh,

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:30.000
what's the IP address of my
web server or my database,

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.880
you can think I want to
allow my web server to talk

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:34.680
to my back end database.

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:37.740
It actually helps to
mitigate user error,

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:42.180
because it's just more natural
to think about it in that form.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:44.520
So, you know, it's an
authenticated rules.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.630
And, you know, obviously, I'm
excited, and I really like it.

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:48.130
OK.

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:52.410
So moving on, just want to
mention private Google access.

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:57.180
This enables you virtual
machines on your subnet

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:00.750
to reach Google API endpoints,
such as BigQuery, cloud

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:04.109
storage, via the Google
network, so you don't actually

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:07.410
have to go out to the
public IP address.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:07.910
OK.

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:13.480
To supplement all
those network controls,

00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:16.390
we had VPC service controls.

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:21.460
VPC service controls allow you
to provide a perimeter boundary

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:23.060
to complement those controls.

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.000
So it's defense in depth.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.030
This isn't a replacement
for any of those.

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:30.920
It's there to help mitigate
data exfiltration risks,

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:33.940
like from misconfiguration
IAM policies.

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:36.580
Those things happen.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:39.620
Maybe you've to make
sure that people

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:42.100
who are allowed to be
accessing your projects

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:43.900
are allow to access
those projects.

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:45.480
So I'll just go for
a quick example,

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.880
so you can visualize
what this actually means.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:53.819
Here we have two projects
that are allowed to talk,

00:17:53.819 --> 00:17:55.110
to communicate with each other.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:56.630
So you've got the IAM controls.

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:59.700
You've got the firewall
rules out between them.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:02.980
But as you can see, there
are other paths in and out.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:05.020
You can have, you know,
an unauthorized client

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:09.130
might be able to get to the
data in your GCP service there.

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:12.580
It might look scary,
but we have something

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:16.820
to kind of alleviate that
pain, service access zones.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:21.280
So here you can see that
you define this perimeter.

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:25.010
You define things like
from trusted IP arranges.

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:29.992
So if I talk you through the
unauthorized client, as if

00:18:29.992 --> 00:18:31.450
[INAUDIBLE] imagine
that you've had

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:33.720
some sort of identity theft.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:34.480
OK?

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:36.730
It's highly unlikely
that they are

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.370
going to be using that
identity to access

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:44.832
your protected project
from your network.

00:18:44.832 --> 00:18:46.540
So it gives you a bit
of time to mitigate

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:50.960
the actual theft of the
identity in that way as well.

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:52.420
You can actually prevent--

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:55.960
using the zone, you can
actually define what

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:57.270
it's not allowed to talk to.

00:18:57.270 --> 00:18:59.140
So you may decide
that these projects

00:18:59.140 --> 00:19:01.930
you don't want them to be able
to write data to another cloud

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:05.770
storage bucket or to BigQuery
outside of those projects.

00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:09.390
So it kind of keeps them
kind of covered up for you.

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.110
OK.

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:13.990
So blending those
together, you have

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:16.420
IAM for the granulated
permissions,

00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:18.580
you have the set of
network controls,

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:20.410
and then you have
VPC service controls

00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:21.580
to complement all of those.

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:27.580
OK.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:30.070
So we've talked about what
your users are allowed to do.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:32.890
Then let's talk about what
they're not allowed to do,

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:36.240
the unauthorized access.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:38.640
You need to manage
who has access.

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:40.810
You need to
understand your users.

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:43.171
So have a central--

00:19:43.171 --> 00:19:44.670
[INAUDIBLE] probably
the wrong word.

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:47.590
Have a central identity store
that you manage your identities

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:48.090
for.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:49.920
So you can use Cloud Identity.

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:52.130
Or, you know, as I
said earlier, you

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:53.700
can use your own identity store.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:58.040
Use groups, as Charlie just
walked you through earlier.

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:00.350
The OS login, this
allows you to tie

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:05.430
in who has SSH login to your
instances with Cloud Identity.

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:07.410
So you're managing
that centrally.

00:20:07.410 --> 00:20:10.920
You give those users
the correct IAM policy.

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.047
They're allowed to
actually SSH in.

00:20:13.047 --> 00:20:14.880
It's a central
administration, so you're not

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:18.360
worrying about actually setting
all that with random keys being

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:22.580
added to instances and metadata.

00:20:22.580 --> 00:20:25.590
User IAM for least privilege.

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:30.780
Stackdriver--
Stackdriver is important,

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.560
because your logs,
your monitoring,

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:36.400
is the first sign of any issues.

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:39.840
So admin activity logs will
tell you who's doing what.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:44.370
Who's made an access call
to a particular sources?

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:45.960
Who started up that VM?

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:49.870
Data access logs-- who's
had access to your data?

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:51.720
You can use Cloud
IAM to restrict who

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:54.650
can actually view those logs.

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:56.460
And then you can do
things like dashboards.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:59.760
You can export them to
your own SIEM if you want.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:00.620
You can filter.

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:04.130
And, obviously, you
can alert on that.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:07.100
Service accounts--
service accounts

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:08.810
are the keys to your door.

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:12.560
So you need to manage
them properly, OK?

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.860
If you're running
on GCE, then we

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:17.630
manage the key rotation for you.

00:21:17.630 --> 00:21:19.160
We manage the rotation.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:20.770
You don't need to download them.

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:23.220
The blast radius is minimized.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:25.724
We do understand that you
do need to download keys.

00:21:25.724 --> 00:21:28.015
So even though I say avoid
downloading production keys,

00:21:28.015 --> 00:21:30.560
I absolutely understand that
you might have applications

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:32.540
that are running
off-prem that you want

00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:36.710
to access GCP API endpoints.

00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:39.320
If you do that, you're going
to have to implement processes

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:41.660
to manage the downloaded keys.

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:49.190
Use things like various tools
to check that your keys aren't

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:50.945
upgraded to GitHub.

00:21:50.945 --> 00:21:52.340
OK?

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:56.900
Restrict, put in policy--
so keys that expire quickly.

00:21:56.900 --> 00:21:59.780
Minimize the radius
and the possibility.

00:21:59.780 --> 00:22:04.010
Use the key management service
to encrypt your config files

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:05.540
and your service account keys.

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:07.580
Your service account
keys are secrets.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:10.280
And then have a process
to unencrypt and use

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:11.845
them and gain audit usage.

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:18.230
So looking at that,
unauthorized access--

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:20.180
again, you've a
number of controls.

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:22.910
And this is just one
way of actually thinking

00:22:22.910 --> 00:22:25.190
of dealing with this-- key
management service, Cloud

00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:27.230
IAM, and Stackdriver logging.

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:30.100
OK.

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:33.610
So now, we talked about
managing and controlling

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:36.170
your GCP environment.

00:22:36.170 --> 00:22:38.510
Now, let's think about
the fact, you know,

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:41.360
you're running an application
on GCP and how you protect that.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:44.920
So let's think about a
web facing application--

00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:47.530
so, again, another
set of controls

00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:50.372
that you can use to
apply to this use case.

00:22:50.372 --> 00:22:53.820
Starting off with
cloud load balancing,

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:56.670
GCP has a number of cloud
load balancing options.

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:58.580
But for our use
case, an application

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:00.120
exposed to the
internet, I'll just

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:04.080
focus on the global
HTTPS load balancer.

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:07.880
When you enable the
HTTPS load balancer,

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:10.170
Google infrastructure
mitigates and absorbs

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:11.820
many layer four
or below attacks,

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:15.540
such as SYN floods, IP fragment
floods, and port exhaustion, et

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:17.290
cetera.

00:23:17.290 --> 00:23:19.180
It's generally
used in conjunction

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:20.510
with managed instance groups.

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:22.060
Managed instant
groups allowing you

00:23:22.060 --> 00:23:25.720
to define a number
of identically

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:28.300
configured instances
that scale up and down.

00:23:28.300 --> 00:23:33.910
So it can absorb any attacks
across those instances

00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:35.520
if you so wish to.

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:37.070
OK?

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:41.550
In conjunction with global load
balancer, we have Cloud Armor.

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:42.050
OK.

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:44.280
And similarly to the
global load balancer--

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:48.260
that's a mouthful-- it's
distributed towards the edge

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:49.640
of Google's network.

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:55.710
So it can absorb attacks
closest to the source.

00:23:55.710 --> 00:24:00.240
It's based on three main things.

00:24:00.240 --> 00:24:03.570
It's built on three pillars--
policy framework, a rich rules

00:24:03.570 --> 00:24:07.890
language, and the global
enforcement infrastructure.

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:10.740
It will help you defend
against DDoS attacks.

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:13.020
You can define
security policies.

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:14.820
You can do whitelists.

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:18.420
You can block traffic.

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:20.460
You can preview traffic.

00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:22.530
It's a very powerful
tool that we've

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:26.550
made available to help you with
your web face and applications.

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:28.832
But it doesn't stop there.

00:24:28.832 --> 00:24:31.190
OK.

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:36.940
Identity Aware Proxy, this has
evolved from Google BeyondCorp.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:41.810
It allows users who have
got the right IAM policies.

00:24:41.810 --> 00:24:46.760
It will authenticate and
authorize access to your web

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:48.060
facing applications.

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:51.600
I'm showing it here working with
Compute Engine and Kubernetes

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:52.230
Engine.

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:56.270
It works pretty much the
same with App Engine.

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:58.170
It's defense in depth
though, so don't

00:24:58.170 --> 00:25:01.260
forget to set your
firewall rules up properly.

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:03.750
And don't forget to make
sure that you're mitigating

00:25:03.750 --> 00:25:05.865
any unauthorized SSH access.

00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:12.340
So we've talked about
your user authentication

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:13.720
and authorization.

00:25:13.720 --> 00:25:16.810
The next thing you said
is what about your APIs.

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:20.590
So an API proxy, think
about it as an abstraction

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:23.860
above your API layer.

00:25:23.860 --> 00:25:26.090
GCP has two types.

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:29.980
It has Cloud Endpoints,
which is great

00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:32.679
if all your application
is all in on GCP.

00:25:32.679 --> 00:25:33.970
It provides a management layer.

00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:37.290
And it will authenticate
calls to the APIs.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:40.050
If, however, you want more
enterprisey type features

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:44.160
and it's multi-platform,
then Apigee API Edge

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:47.580
will be the one that you
should be looking at.

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:53.160
So IAP is for your
users, and API proxies

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:57.330
for your authentication
of your APIs.

00:25:57.330 --> 00:25:58.800
And your application code--

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:01.282
and you need to be secure,
and you need to trust it.

00:26:01.282 --> 00:26:02.430
OK?

00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:07.650
So-- user security
scanner for GCE and GAE.

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:11.770
Make sure you're mitigating
for the OWASP top ten.

00:26:11.770 --> 00:26:12.800
Know your code.

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:14.930
So use private source repos.

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:17.550
Contain a registry
and contain a builder.

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:20.510
Know what's in your code
that you're deploying.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:26.050
So from one end to
the other-- there's

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:28.819
the Identity Aware Proxy if
your users are accessing,

00:26:28.819 --> 00:26:30.610
Cloud Armor in conjunction
with global load

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:33.560
balancer to mitigate,
help block traffic

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:35.650
and help mitigate
on DDoS attacks,

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:41.370
and then API proxies for
your APIs, all right?

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:43.110
So finally, and
not least, I just

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:47.630
want to talk about
watching what's going on.

00:26:47.630 --> 00:26:49.800
So monitoring,
alerting, and enforcing.

00:26:49.800 --> 00:26:55.424
So as you expect, we've got
a collection of tools again.

00:26:55.424 --> 00:26:57.590
And as before, I'm just
going to talk about the ones

00:26:57.590 --> 00:26:59.620
that I haven't
already spoke about.

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:03.210
So let's just have a quick
look at VPC flow logs.

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:06.050
There was a great talk
yesterday from Ines, the PM--

00:27:06.050 --> 00:27:07.690
so if you didn't
catch it, then really

00:27:07.690 --> 00:27:10.240
go and watch it on YouTube--

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:11.650
all about VPC flow logs.

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:14.242
They allow you to
get network telemetry

00:27:14.242 --> 00:27:17.740
for to and from your VM whether
within GCP or whether it's

00:27:17.740 --> 00:27:22.600
coming from the internet
going to your VMs.

00:27:22.600 --> 00:27:25.960
It's useful for forensics
and security analysis.

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:29.230
You can export to an SIEM.

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:32.350
You can view this telemetry
data within Stackdriver.

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:34.930
So you can export
to any of the syncs

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:37.825
that Stackdriver supports, like
BigQuery and Cloud Storage.

00:27:41.030 --> 00:27:42.620
Cloud Security
Command Center, this

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:44.830
gives you a single
pane of glass.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:45.530
OK?

00:27:45.530 --> 00:27:50.030
It gives you a picture, a
security slanted picture,

00:27:50.030 --> 00:27:51.950
of your resources.

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:53.240
It's modular.

00:27:53.240 --> 00:27:53.900
OK?

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:56.450
It gives you an asset
discovery and inventory.

00:27:56.450 --> 00:27:58.127
What's really cool
about the inventory?

00:27:58.127 --> 00:27:59.710
You know, you're
working in the cloud,

00:27:59.710 --> 00:28:02.604
so you're going to be
scaling up and down.

00:28:02.604 --> 00:28:04.520
You know, there might
have been some instances

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:06.860
in the past that have
been used to do, you know,

00:28:06.860 --> 00:28:07.640
Bitcoin mining.

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:09.620
That's a big thing
at the moment.

00:28:09.620 --> 00:28:11.900
Those were spun
up, then spun down.

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:15.200
You can still have insights into
those spun down or destroyed

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:16.656
instances.

00:28:16.656 --> 00:28:19.666
OK, that's kind of a really nice
feature of the Cloud Security

00:28:19.666 --> 00:28:20.290
Command Center.

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:21.830
It's got lots of
other nice features,

00:28:21.830 --> 00:28:23.746
but that's the one that
I really, really love.

00:28:23.746 --> 00:28:26.890
It integrates with the
data loss prevention API.

00:28:26.890 --> 00:28:31.220
Data loss prevention API
allows you to classify

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:32.494
your sensitive data.

00:28:32.494 --> 00:28:34.160
If you want more
information about that,

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:36.920
there is the showcase
where the DLP

00:28:36.920 --> 00:28:38.750
engineers are hanging around.

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:43.850
Cloud Security Scanner--
to scan your applications.

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:47.530
It allows you to integrate
with third party tools as well.

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:49.250
And it gives you notifications.

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:52.480
So Forseti is one of those
tools it integrates with.

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:54.980
Forseti is open
source security tool.

00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:58.070
And that's evolved from GCP's--

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:00.800
evolved from Google's
internal tooling.

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:02.000
It's community driven.

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:04.850
It's open source,
takes pull requests.

00:29:04.850 --> 00:29:06.620
It gives you an inventory.

00:29:06.620 --> 00:29:09.840
It allows you to
enforce firewall rules.

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:11.870
So if, for example, you
have a standard that

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:14.480
says this is the firewall
rules that I want to apply,

00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:16.570
somebody sets it back.

00:29:16.570 --> 00:29:19.760
Forseti will actually
reset that for you.

00:29:19.760 --> 00:29:21.620
IAM Explain is
really kind of cool,

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:24.920
because it allows you
to understand the impact

00:29:24.920 --> 00:29:28.220
of your IAM policies.

00:29:28.220 --> 00:29:32.420
So the Forseti guys are
also around here as well.

00:29:32.420 --> 00:29:34.450
So blending that together--

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:39.610
VPC flow logs for your network
telemetry, Cloud Security

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:42.640
Command Center for your single
pane of glass, Stackdriver

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:46.060
logging for who did what, where,
and when, and for the alerts.

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:48.840
OK.

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:51.150
So I'd now like to
introduce Arthur

00:29:51.150 --> 00:29:53.460
from Verily, who's going
to talk to you about how

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:56.190
they watched what's going on.

00:29:56.190 --> 00:29:57.229
Thanks, Arthur.

00:29:57.229 --> 00:29:58.726
Thanks, Grace.

00:29:58.726 --> 00:30:03.095
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:03.095 --> 00:30:04.220
ARTHUR BRANT: Hi, everyone.

00:30:04.220 --> 00:30:05.230
My name is Arthur.

00:30:05.230 --> 00:30:08.200
And I'm a software engineer
at Verily Life Sciences.

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:10.810
I work closely with Charlie
building the services,

00:30:10.810 --> 00:30:14.750
tooling, and scripting to keep
our health care data secure.

00:30:14.750 --> 00:30:17.110
So my section of
the talk will be

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:20.050
diving into the weeds a
little bit on specific things

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:22.420
that we've done to
abide by best practices

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:25.280
to keep the data as
secure as possible.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:27.250
So there will be
three big principles

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:28.850
that we speak about today.

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:33.760
One is how to generate,
persist, and reasonably analyze

00:30:33.760 --> 00:30:36.040
your audit logs
to look at who is

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:39.670
accessing what data and
security configurations.

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:44.050
Two is looking at how
to reasonably perform

00:30:44.050 --> 00:30:46.672
org-wide IAM policy reviews.

00:30:46.672 --> 00:30:48.880
You need to know what's in
your policies to make sure

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:50.380
that they're secure.

00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:53.079
And third is how you can
form company-wide roll outs

00:30:53.079 --> 00:30:55.120
and patches in the event
that there is a security

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:58.160
vulnerability that
you've identified.

00:30:58.160 --> 00:31:02.650
So we'll first
dive into logging.

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:04.180
In health care,
this is something

00:31:04.180 --> 00:31:06.490
that we're legally required to
do for the most sensitive types

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:07.090
of data.

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:08.830
And it's generally
a best practice

00:31:08.830 --> 00:31:10.210
that we should all be doing.

00:31:10.210 --> 00:31:12.430
And there's some questions
that you may want to ask.

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:15.460
For example, for a particular
piece of sensitive data,

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:17.510
who all or what are
all of the identities

00:31:17.510 --> 00:31:19.640
that access that piece of data?

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:22.270
Second, you may
want to look at who

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:23.959
is setting security
configurations

00:31:23.959 --> 00:31:25.750
in your project in the
event that something

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:27.430
was opened up too widely?

00:31:27.430 --> 00:31:30.130
And third is you may
want to due diligence

00:31:30.130 --> 00:31:34.510
on a particular employee and
ask what are every piece of data

00:31:34.510 --> 00:31:37.540
or every security configuration
update that, you know,

00:31:37.540 --> 00:31:39.880
so and so has done
at your company.

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:41.680
Those are questions you may ask.

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:43.750
So there's three
big things that you

00:31:43.750 --> 00:31:46.510
need to guarantee when using
Google Cloud Platform to make

00:31:46.510 --> 00:31:50.170
sure that you can answer
these questions reliably.

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:52.630
Number one is you need to
turn on your audit logs.

00:31:52.630 --> 00:31:55.450
By default, your security
configuration update

00:31:55.450 --> 00:31:57.760
logs are on, but your
data read and data write

00:31:57.760 --> 00:31:59.630
logs are not on for projects.

00:31:59.630 --> 00:32:03.000
So it's imperative that you set
these on at the project level

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:05.405
IAM policy and that you also--

00:32:05.405 --> 00:32:07.030
we'll talk about this
a little bit more

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:09.690
downstream, but-- that you check
that they're never flipped off

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:11.110
ephemerally.

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:14.260
Second is that you need
to export your logs.

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:16.060
Now, the natural
place that someone

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:18.760
may try to export the logs is
within the project for which

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:20.300
the logs are being generated.

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:23.170
However, we'll talk here
shortly on why that's

00:32:23.170 --> 00:32:25.420
a very bad idea actually.

00:32:25.420 --> 00:32:27.430
You may export your
logs through a couple

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:30.000
of different services on GCP.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:32.040
GCS exports to JSON.

00:32:32.040 --> 00:32:36.040
It's great for data robustness,
not the best for doing analysis

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:37.180
on your logs.

00:32:37.180 --> 00:32:38.980
They also export
to BigQuery, which

00:32:38.980 --> 00:32:42.580
is our preference and probably
the easiest place to really

00:32:42.580 --> 00:32:43.852
dive into those logs.

00:32:43.852 --> 00:32:46.060
And then if you need to
build some real time business

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:47.560
logic on top of
your audit logs, you

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:50.740
can also export
to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:32:50.740 --> 00:32:54.670
Now, the reason that I was
speaking of earlier on why

00:32:54.670 --> 00:32:59.590
not to create a BigQuery set
in a project itself for which

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:01.290
the logs are going
to be dumped to

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:05.290
is because, oftentimes,
we set wide access rules

00:33:05.290 --> 00:33:08.200
at the project level
IAM policy, and IAM

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:09.950
is fundamentally hierarchical.

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:12.520
So, for example, if you use
project editor, BigQuery

00:33:12.520 --> 00:33:17.500
dataset editor, BigQuery
admin, storage object admin,

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:20.110
storage object
delete permissions,

00:33:20.110 --> 00:33:22.479
you're going to be giving
a potentially wide group

00:33:22.479 --> 00:33:24.520
of people access to
permanently delete your audit

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:27.880
logs with no means
of reproducing them.

00:33:27.880 --> 00:33:29.960
And if you're in the
business of health care,

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:32.860
that's something that we
absolutely cannot have.

00:33:32.860 --> 00:33:35.360
So instead, we've adopted
a different best practice,

00:33:35.360 --> 00:33:40.960
which is to export logs
into a company-wide project

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:44.410
where no individual has
24/7 access to this project.

00:33:44.410 --> 00:33:46.930
And we can ensure that
our logs are never

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:50.770
deleted by the teams for
which we want to be auditing.

00:33:50.770 --> 00:33:54.660
Another key detail on this
slide is that you can see--

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:57.260
and Charlie spoke a little
bit more about this earlier--

00:33:57.260 --> 00:34:00.160
we are pretty heavily
using cloud folders

00:34:00.160 --> 00:34:03.670
to segregate different
clusters of projects

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:06.010
into different
hierarchical paths.

00:34:06.010 --> 00:34:08.531
And this is very nice,
because now Cloud Folders

00:34:08.531 --> 00:34:10.239
has a feature where
you can turn on audit

00:34:10.239 --> 00:34:11.929
logging for all children.

00:34:11.929 --> 00:34:14.860
So as long as the projects
are generating the logs

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:17.739
and the folder export is
configured appropriately,

00:34:17.739 --> 00:34:20.080
you can have strong guarantees
that every single audit

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:26.020
log underneath some parent
label or parent folder

00:34:26.020 --> 00:34:27.969
will be exported somewhere.

00:34:27.969 --> 00:34:31.210
And for us, we actually have 1
to 1 parity between our cloud

00:34:31.210 --> 00:34:33.820
folders and our
BigQuery datasets

00:34:33.820 --> 00:34:37.810
in a company-wide super
privileged project.

00:34:37.810 --> 00:34:40.521
And this will come
into handy later.

00:34:40.521 --> 00:34:41.020
OK.

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:43.630
So the third principle
with audit logs

00:34:43.630 --> 00:34:45.699
is that people need to
actually look at them

00:34:45.699 --> 00:34:47.650
or you need to
run automated jobs

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:49.389
to check for specific things.

00:34:49.389 --> 00:34:53.440
For example, who all has changed
your project level IAM policy

00:34:53.440 --> 00:34:55.900
in the last month?

00:34:55.900 --> 00:34:57.520
What individual
non-service accounts

00:34:57.520 --> 00:35:01.510
have accessed data that should
never be accessed by humans?

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:03.310
And the way that we've
configured things

00:35:03.310 --> 00:35:04.900
that you saw in
the last slide is

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:06.370
that, because
there's this 1 to 1

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:10.300
parity between a cloud folder
and a BigQuery dataset,

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:13.630
you can simply grant access
to whatever groups have break

00:35:13.630 --> 00:35:16.210
glass access or super
admin access or TL

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:19.600
access on the cluster of
projects to their own audit

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:21.940
logs with BigQuery
Data Set Viewer,

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:24.940
so that they can read the
logs, and BigQuery job user,

00:35:24.940 --> 00:35:27.580
so that they can actually
run queries against them.

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:29.860
This ensures that they
can look at their logs,

00:35:29.860 --> 00:35:32.350
but also ensures that your
logs will never be deleted.

00:35:32.350 --> 00:35:34.980
And you won't be liable
for that as a company.

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:35.480
OK.

00:35:35.480 --> 00:35:39.430
Second big topic for
the day is looking

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:41.560
at how we can
perform IAM security

00:35:41.560 --> 00:35:44.080
audits and some specific
use cases for that.

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:45.580
Going back to the
previous slide,

00:35:45.580 --> 00:35:47.870
if we look at the
clusters of folders,

00:35:47.870 --> 00:35:52.520
we can imagine that if a TL is
responsible for some cluster.

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:54.130
Whether it's the
data warehouse, you

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:58.440
know, the front end serving
app, the replicas in one folder,

00:35:58.440 --> 00:36:01.470
they want to be able to
look at every single IAM

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:03.660
policy or some IAM
policy [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:05.880
for their set of projects.

00:36:05.880 --> 00:36:07.650
Specific things you
may want to check

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:10.590
is that an identity outside
of your organization

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:13.540
does not have access to
sensitive information.

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:17.580
So, for example, if you're a
company that hires a consultant

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:22.180
and you decide to use that
consultant's @gmail.com address

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:24.150
and at some point
the deal is done

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:27.070
and you want to
remove their access,

00:36:27.070 --> 00:36:29.280
this can actually be a
very complicated project.

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:30.930
You can't simply
delete the account,

00:36:30.930 --> 00:36:32.680
because it may be
their personal Gmail.

00:36:32.680 --> 00:36:36.690
So instead, you need to scrape
every policy recursively

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:37.500
and delete them.

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:41.880
And the only really way to
guarantee that is via this,

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:44.490
I think, the folder structure
and recursively crawling down

00:36:44.490 --> 00:36:46.590
and updating the IAM policies.

00:36:46.590 --> 00:36:50.790
Second big thing is you may want
to do routine audits to make

00:36:50.790 --> 00:36:54.060
sure that only robot
identities have access

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:56.710
to some of the sensitive
data in your project--

00:36:56.710 --> 00:37:00.360
so whitelists, specific
buckets, specific data sets,

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:02.610
specific SQL
instances, and ensure

00:37:02.610 --> 00:37:05.670
that no one's granted
themselves access to look at it.

00:37:05.670 --> 00:37:08.002
Third is, as we were
talking about earlier,

00:37:08.002 --> 00:37:09.960
you have to make sure
that all of your projects

00:37:09.960 --> 00:37:12.000
have audit logging on
and that this is never

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:13.200
turned off by a user.

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:15.524
So we should be auditing this.

00:37:15.524 --> 00:37:17.190
And fourth, and this
is a problem that's

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:20.460
plagued pretty much all
of the cloud providers,

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:23.070
is that there is a
feature on GSC Buckets

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:26.850
and BigQuery data sets where you
can make the data within them

00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:29.610
readable to the entire
world without requiring

00:37:29.610 --> 00:37:30.780
any authorization.

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:33.659
It's great for global
research collaborations,

00:37:33.659 --> 00:37:35.700
but you need to make sure
it's never on your most

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:36.780
sensitive projects.

00:37:36.780 --> 00:37:39.690
So we, at Verily, have some
very robust tooling around this

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:42.750
to make sure that it
will never happen to us.

00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:43.590
OK.

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.470
Third topic that we
want to talk about today

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:50.640
is how to perform company-wide
roll outs in cases

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:52.890
of time sensitive emergencies.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:55.530
Again, the folder
structure is really

00:37:55.530 --> 00:37:57.910
paramount to performing this.

00:37:57.910 --> 00:38:02.400
So, for example,
one use case we had,

00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:04.050
which is the first
one listed there,

00:38:04.050 --> 00:38:06.870
was there were
three times in 2017

00:38:06.870 --> 00:38:09.720
where we needed to patch
every single VM in the company

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:13.020
due to bugs, like the
global Spectre bug, which

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:18.180
are bugs that effect the OS
machines that Google doesn't

00:38:18.180 --> 00:38:22.270
control for your VMs.

00:38:22.270 --> 00:38:24.550
And to orchestrate
this smoothly,

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:26.890
the client is actually
responsible for rebooting

00:38:26.890 --> 00:38:29.140
and patching every VM,
because Google is not

00:38:29.140 --> 00:38:31.150
going to randomly turn
down your machines.

00:38:31.150 --> 00:38:36.560
It could affect
production access.

00:38:36.560 --> 00:38:38.870
So by having the
cloud folders, we

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:40.540
are able to look
at all the folders

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:42.280
that don't serve
production traffic,

00:38:42.280 --> 00:38:45.910
immediately roll out a job to
reboot and patch their VMs,

00:38:45.910 --> 00:38:48.550
and then go team by team for
ones that did serve production

00:38:48.550 --> 00:38:50.980
traffic and, with the
consent of their TLs,

00:38:50.980 --> 00:38:53.750
recursively reboot all
of their VMs for them.

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:55.870
And as a result, we
as a company were

00:38:55.870 --> 00:38:59.380
able to reboot thousands of
VMs in a couple of hours.

00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:01.180
Whereas, if you don't
have this structure,

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:02.930
it would be a nightmare
to even track down

00:39:02.930 --> 00:39:04.880
the VMs in the first place.

00:39:04.880 --> 00:39:09.910
Second case where this is
particularly useful is,

00:39:09.910 --> 00:39:13.210
because we have a service
account with super admin

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:17.350
access at the top, we can
pretty easily add people in

00:39:17.350 --> 00:39:20.200
as long as there's a couple
approvals to grant break glass

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:23.080
access to, for example,
security engineers that

00:39:23.080 --> 00:39:26.350
find some mission critical
configuration issue

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:28.330
or other people on
a team in the event

00:39:28.330 --> 00:39:30.670
that the TL forgot
to, you know, set up

00:39:30.670 --> 00:39:32.770
the on-call group
appropriately, and we

00:39:32.770 --> 00:39:36.010
need to give someone
access to the project.

00:39:36.010 --> 00:39:40.450
And the third example here,
which we talked about earlier,

00:39:40.450 --> 00:39:44.860
was being able to quickly
crawl your entire GCP universe

00:39:44.860 --> 00:39:49.590
and remove a specific
identity from all access.

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:50.090
OK.

00:39:50.090 --> 00:39:52.780
So at this point, I'd like
to hand it back to Grace.

00:39:52.780 --> 00:39:56.290
And we spoke a little bit about
how Verily uses the best of GCP

00:39:56.290 --> 00:39:58.720
to secure our
hypersensitive data.

00:39:58.720 --> 00:39:59.560
Thank you, Grace.

00:39:59.560 --> 00:40:00.976
GRACE MOLLISON:
Thank you, Arthur.

00:40:00.976 --> 00:40:06.300
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:06.920
OK.

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:10.310
Just to wrap up and
then for some Q&amp;A--

00:40:10.310 --> 00:40:12.760
so just a checklist.

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:14.240
You need to define.

00:40:14.240 --> 00:40:18.140
When you start off thinking
about what controls you need,

00:40:18.140 --> 00:40:21.170
define what your user
should be able to do.

00:40:21.170 --> 00:40:25.310
Think about those controls,
the IAM map, functional map.

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:28.570
Think about what's not
allowed, organization policy.

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:31.070
And then think about what you're
trying to guard and protect

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:32.000
against.

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:32.500
OK.

00:40:32.500 --> 00:40:33.420
What are the threats?

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:35.750
What are your concerns?

00:40:35.750 --> 00:40:37.610
Choose your controls
from the shopping list.

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:39.170
Remember, these
are just a subset.

00:40:39.170 --> 00:40:42.050
And I've just kind of put
them into a logical order.

00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:44.300
Blend those controls together
for a defense and depth

00:40:44.300 --> 00:40:46.190
approach, and monitor
all the things.

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:48.340
